dImages">

		=09
		=09

				<p>
	<span style=3D"font-size:20px;">Viewspeed v2</span>
</p>

<p>
	&nbsp;
</p>

<p>
	<a href=3D"https://www.mouse-sensitivity.com/profile/5312-dnamte/" rel=3D"=
">DNAMTE</a> has come up with another solution for converting sensitivity t=
o different field of views. This time it is independent of the aspect ratio=
, which is a flaw with viewspeed v1. The aim of this solution is to make th=
e different field of views behave the same visually when moving the mouse, =
solving any perceptual issues such as feeling like it is slowing down or sp=
eeding up. This makes this method especially useful for zoom/ads sensitivit=
ies.
</p>

<p>
	&nbsp;
</p>

<p>
	The sensitivity scaling is done using two ratios, the circle ratio and the=
 chord ratio.
</p>

<p>
	<i-lightbox data-wrappedlink=3D"" data-ipslightbox=3D"" href=3D"http://i.i=
mgur.com/Psp1Wh9.png" data-fileid=3D"undefined" data-ipslightbox-group=3D"g=
81098"><img alt=3D"Psp1Wh9.png" class=3D"ipsImage" height=3D"546" src=3D"ht=
tp://i.imgur.com/Psp1Wh9.png" width=3D"1000" loading=3D"lazy" title=3D"=E6=
=94=BE=E5=A4=A7=E5=9C=96=E7=89=87"></i-lightbox></p>

<p>
	&nbsp;
</p>

<p>
	DNAMTE is unsure on how to do the math for it, so I will do it. In the pic=
ture above, he did 90 degrees and 45 degrees for the angle of view, and fou=
nd a chord ratio of 2.41 and a circle ratio of 1.3.
</p>

<pre class=3D"ipsCode language-undefined hljs" data-ips-text-highlighted=3D=
"yes"><code>circle ratio / chord ratio</code></pre>

<p>
	The chord ratio can be found using:
</p>

<pre class=3D"ipsCode language-lua hljs" data-ips-text-highlighted=3D"yes">=
<code><span class=3D"hljs-built_in">tan</span>(y * <span class=3D"hljs-buil=
t_in">pi</span>/<span class=3D"hljs-number">360</span>) / <span class=3D"hl=
js-built_in">tan</span>(x * <span class=3D"hljs-built_in">pi</span>/<span c=
lass=3D"hljs-number">360</span>)</code></pre>

<p>
	Where the variables are the degrees. If we substitute x for 45, and y for =
90, we end up with a ratio of 2.41 as shown in the image above.
</p>

<p>
	Scaling the sensitivity by the chord ratio alone would be equal to scaling=
 the sensitivity by the zoom amount.
</p>

<p>
	&nbsp;
</p>

<p>
	The circle ratio can be found using:
</p>

<pre class=3D"ipsCode language-lua hljs" data-ips-text-highlighted=3D"yes">=
<code><span class=3D"hljs-built_in">cos</span>(x * <span class=3D"hljs-buil=
t_in">pi</span>/<span class=3D"hljs-number">360</span>) / <span class=3D"hl=
js-built_in">cos</span>(y * <span class=3D"hljs-built_in">pi</span>/<span c=
lass=3D"hljs-number">360</span>)</code></pre>

<p>
	If we substitute x for 45, and y for 90, we end up with a ratio of 1.3 as =
shown in the image above.
</p>

<p>
	&nbsp;
</p>

<p>
	Put these two together, and you simply get the sin ratio.
</p>

<pre class=3D"ipsCode prettyprint lang-html prettyprinted language-xml hljs=
" data-ips-text-highlighted=3D"yes"><code>(cos(x * pi/360) / cos(y * pi/360=
)) / (tan(y * pi/360) / tan(x * pi/360))
=3D sin(x * pi/360) / sin(y * pi/360)</code></pre>

<p>
	&nbsp;
</p>

<p>
	When substituting the variables with the angles, you want to use the verti=
cal / horizontal angle for the 1:1 aspect ratio.
</p>

<p>
	&nbsp;
</p>

<p>
	With this additional option of converting sensitivities, you end up with t=
he options of converting using the tan ratio (mm 0%), sin ratio (viewspeed =
v2), or matching a screen-space distance using monitor distance match.
</p>

<p>
	&nbsp;
</p>

<p>
	Recommendations:
</p>

<ul><li>
		Use tan ratio (0%) or 360 distance match for converting hipfire.
	</li>
	<li>
		Use tan ratio (0%) or sin ratio (viewspeed) for converting zoom/ads.
	</li>
</ul>

